Use pressure and thermal models with explicit scales and assumptions (@geo-thermal-earth: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @geology, #source "../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX", #source "../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW", #source "../../../../stem/chemistry/general-chemistry/General-chemistry.lingua#r_206GS6CVXD8WSY1VX7PRF7136V", #geology "Isostasy-elevation-and-lithospheric-support.lingua#r_5Z4DP3BN9EEQEZW8R86704E3FY") { r_1K4DA43MK7DGA9Q05RFF1WFWJJ
**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), interior temperature and metamorphic controls; [Geological Structures: a Practical Introduction — Waldron and Snyder](../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW), stress and material-behavior sections; [General chemistry](../../../../stem/chemistry/general-chemistry/General-chemistry.lingua#r_206GS6CVXD8WSY1VX7PRF7136V), thermodynamic and phase foundations where needed.

An approximate lithostatic pressure account integrates density and gravity with depth. With invented constant density 2700 kg m⁻³, g = 9.81 m s⁻², and depth 10 km, p−p_surface = ρgz gives 264.87 MPa. This is a teaching model; actual stress, density, topography, fluids, and depth-reference conditions can require more information.

A geothermal gradient summarizes a spatial temperature change over a range, not every region's full thermal history. A hypothetical 25 K km⁻¹ gradient from a 15 °C surface gives 265 °C at 10 km under that linear model. Magmatism, fluid flow, deformation, burial/exhumation, and transient heat transfer can alter the interpretation.

For a conduction model a characteristic time scale L²/κ shows why size matters. With invented κ = 10⁻⁶ m² s⁻¹ and L = 1 km, the scale is 10¹² s, about 31,700 Julian years. It is a scaling estimate rather than an exact cooling time for an arbitrary body; boundary conditions, geometry, and changing properties matter.

**Work:** derive each relation with units, check the examples independently, and analyze how doubling length changes the thermal time scale. Compare pressure/stress and temperature/history claims in a metamorphic or igneous interpretation. Identify what measurements and model would be required to replace the simple estimates.

Read the relevant complete interior, metamorphic, and structural sections. These calculations connect geology with chemistry, heat transfer, and mechanics while leaving the specialist theories available for deeper study.

